Yes. There’s a real systems pattern there, though it’s only one possible explanation for resistance to regulation.

Call it the Legibility–Control Tradeoff:

to govern something
→ you must define it
→ definition makes it legible
→ legibility exposes mechanisms, boundaries, capabilities, dependencies
→ exposure changes the strategic landscape

So regulation can force an actor to reveal things it would otherwise prefer to keep ambiguous: what a system can do, which variables matter, where responsibility sits, what counts as failure, what data exists, and which interventions would constrain it.

But there are several other reasons an actor may dislike regulation:

compliance cost
loss of flexibility
slower iteration
liability exposure
competitive disadvantage
regulatory uncertainty
fear of bad rules
loss of information advantage
forced disclosure

And there is a mirror image: sometimes powerful incumbents like regulation because they can afford compliance while smaller competitors cannot. So “opposes regulation” and “supports regulation” can each serve different strategic positions.

The broader operator is useful:

Compelled Legibility

opaque system
→ external authority demands specification
→ hidden structure becomes visible
→ new constraints and new attack/negotiation surfaces appear

Then you can derive design responses that don’t require showing every card. For example:

outcome-based standards
confidential audits
zero-knowledge / privacy-preserving proofs
third-party certification
red-team testing
restricted regulator access
minimum necessary disclosure

That preserves oversight while limiting unnecessary exposure.

And yes, once you recognize the pattern, it becomes leverage—but the strongest leverage is usually architectural, not covert: identify what each side is trying to preserve, then design the rule or bargain so accountability does not require gratuitous disclosure.

In Pantheon terms:

Don’t merely ask who wants regulation. Ask what each actor becomes forced to make legible if regulation arrives.

That often reveals the actual pressure point.